10. Object.assign() -> Object.assign() copies enumerable properties from one or more source objects into a target object.

Object.assign() एक या अधिक source objects की properties को target object में copy करने के लिए उपयोग किया जाता है।

Syntax
Object.assign(target, source);

Practical Example
const student = {
    name: "Adyut"
};

const details = {
    age: 21,
    course: "BCA"
};

Object.assign(student, details);
console.log(student);

Output:
{
    name: "Adyut",
    age: 21,
    course: "BCA"
}

student = Target Object
details = Source Object

properties of details
        ↓
copy in student

11. Object.create() -> Object.create() creates a new object using another object as its prototype.
Object.create() एक नया object बनाता है और दिए गए object को उसके prototype के रूप में set करता है।

Syntax
Object.create(prototype);

Practical Example
const person = {
    greet: function() {
        console.log("Hello");
    }
};

const student = Object.create(person);
student.name = "Adyut";
console.log(student.name);
student.greet();

Output:Adyut Hello
English: student does not directly contain greet(), but it can access it through its prototype.


12. Object.fromEntries() -> Object.fromEntries() creates an object from an iterable containing key-value pairs.

Object.fromEntries() key-value pairs की list से एक नया object बनाता है।

Example
const entries = [
    ["name", "Adyut"],
    ["age", 21],
    ["course", "BCA"]
];

const student = Object.fromEntries(entries);
console.log(student);

Output:
{
    name: "Adyut",
    age: 21,
    course: "BCA"
}
Easy Concept
[["name", "Adyut"],
 ["age", 21]]
        ↓
Object.fromEntries()
        ↓
{name: "Adyut", age: 21}

